---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const topics = [
  {
    week: 'Week 23',
    date: 'Jun 6, 2026',
    title: 'Month 1: CS224W — Machine Learning with Graphs',
    description:
      'First month only: finish the CS224W series. Plan is 20 lecture slots, one lecture per day, and no other readings for now.',
    links: [
      { label: 'Course content', href: 'https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs224w/index.html#content' },
      { label: 'Schedule', href: 'https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs224w/index.html#schedule' },
      { label: 'Notes PDF', href: 'https://archives.leni.sh/stanford/CS224w.pdf' },
    ],
  },
];

const lectures = [
  'Introduction',
  'Node embeddings',
  'Graph neural networks',
  'General perspective on GNNs',
  'GNN augmentation and training',
  'Theory of GNNs',
  'Powerful graph encoders',
  'Graph Transformers',
  'Heterogeneous graphs',
  'Knowledge graphs',
  'GNNs for recommender systems',
  'Relational Deep Learning',
  'Advanced architectures in RDL',
  'Advanced topics in GNNs',
  'Foundation models for Knowledge Graphs',
  'LLM + GNN',
  'Agents + Graphs',
  'Deep generative models for graphs',
  'Conclusion',
  'Recap / buffer',
];
---
<Base title="IR Reading Group" description="One-page overview for the IR Reading Group.">
  <h1>IR Reading Group</h1>
  <p>
    A one-page overview of what we are reading. Month 1 is fully dedicated to Stanford CS224W,
    <em>Machine Learning with Graphs</em>.
  </p>

  <div class="toolbar" aria-label="Topic list controls">
    <div class="search-label">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
        <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"></circle>
        <path d="m20 20-4-4"></path>
      </svg>
      <span>Search topics...</span>
    </div>
    <div class="sort-label">Oldest first</div>
  </div>

  <section class="topic-list" aria-label="Reading group topics">
    {topics.map((topic) => (
      <article class="topic-row">
        <div class="week">
          <div>{topic.week}</div>
          <div>{topic.date}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="topic">
          <h2>{topic.title}</h2>
          <p>{topic.description}</p>
          <div class="links">
            {topic.links.map((link) => (
              <span><a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a></span>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="month-plan" aria-label="Month 1 lecture plan">
    <h2>Month 1 lecture plan</h2>
    <div class="lecture-grid">
      {lectures.map((lecture, index) => (
        <div class="lecture-cell">
          <span>Lecture {index + 1}</span>
          <strong>{lecture}</strong>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="db-internals" aria-label="Database Internals notes">
    <h2>Database Internals — Chapter 1 Notes</h2>
    <p class="db-intro">
      Database bahar se simple lagta hai, but andar bilkul khatarnaak game chal raha hai — data ko kaise store karna, kaise jaldi dhoondhna, aur crash ke baad kaise bachana.
    </p>

    <div class="db-grid">

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>DBMS Architecture</h3>
        <p>Query ka flow layers cross karta hai before disk pe jaane se:</p>
        <div class="flow-chain">
          <span>Client</span>
          <span>Transport</span>
          <span>Query Processor</span>
          <span>Query Optimizer</span>
          <span>Execution Engine</span>
          <span>Storage Engine</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>Storage Engine Components</h3>
        <ul class="db-list">
          <li><strong>Transaction Manager</strong> — ensures atomicity; ya poora commit, ya rollback</li>
          <li><strong>Lock Manager</strong> — concurrent users ke beech race conditions se bachata hai</li>
          <li><strong>Access Methods</strong> — B-Tree, LSM Tree, Hash Index, Heap File</li>
          <li><strong>Buffer Manager</strong> — disk slow hai, RAM fast — pages cache karta hai</li>
          <li><strong>Recovery Manager</strong> — crash ke baad WAL se redo/undo decide karta hai</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>Memory vs Disk DB</h3>
        <ul class="db-list">
          <li>Disk DB mein data disk pe, RAM sirf cache ke liye</li>
          <li>Memory DB mein data RAM mein, disk sirf logs/snapshots ke liye</li>
          <li>In-memory DB sirf "disk DB with huge cache" <em>nahi</em> hota — internal structure fundamentally different hota hai</li>
          <li>Memory DB durability ke liye WAL + snapshots + checkpointing use karta hai</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>Row vs Column Layout</h3>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-col">
            <div class="compare-label row-label">Row Store</div>
            <p>Full row saath stored. OLTP ke liye best — single user fetch, payment update, login.</p>
            <div class="examples">PostgreSQL · MySQL · SQLite</div>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-col">
            <div class="compare-label col-label">Column Store</div>
            <p>Same column saath stored. Analytics ke liye best — AVG, SUM, dashboard scans.</p>
            <div class="examples">ClickHouse · Parquet · ORC</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>Data File Types</h3>
        <ul class="db-list">
          <li><strong>Heap File</strong> — insert order mein, fast writes, lookup ke liye index chahiye</li>
          <li><strong>Hash-organized</strong> — exact lookup fast, range scan weak (hash order destroy karta hai)</li>
          <li><strong>Index-organized Table</strong> — data primary key order mein, range scan + lookup dono achhe</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>Index Types</h3>
        <ul class="db-list">
          <li><strong>Primary Index</strong> — primary key pe, usually unique</li>
          <li><strong>Secondary Index</strong> — non-primary field pe, duplicates allowed</li>
          <li><strong>Clustered</strong> — physical data order = index key order, range scan fast</li>
          <li><strong>Nonclustered</strong> — index sorted but data file us order mein nahi</li>
          <li><strong>Indirection trick</strong> — secondary index physical location store karne ki jagah primary key store karta hai, maintenance easy hoti hai</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card">
        <h3>Three Big Ideas</h3>
        <ul class="db-list">
          <li><strong>Buffering</strong> — small writes collect karo, ek large sequential write karo. Crash safety ke liye WAL.</li>
          <li><strong>Immutability</strong> — old file modify nahi, new version likho. Concurrent reads safe, compaction needed.</li>
          <li><strong>Ordering</strong> — data key order mein rakho. Range scans fast, but writes harder.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:0.8rem; font-size:0.95rem; color: var(--muted);">Tombstones: delete = marker likhna, actual removal compaction mein hota hai.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="db-card db-card--wide">
        <h3>The Real Lesson — Trade-offs</h3>
        <div class="tradeoff-grid">
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Row store</span><em>OLTP ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; analytics ✗</em></div>
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Column store</span><em>analytics ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; full-row fetch ✗</em></div>
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Hash file</span><em>exact lookup ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; range scan ✗</em></div>
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Heap file</span><em>insert easy ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; needs index ✗</em></div>
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Memory DB</span><em>fast ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; durability tricky ✗</em></div>
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Immutable storage</span><em>writes easy ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; compaction cost ✗</em></div>
          <div class="tradeoff-item"><span>Ordered storage</span><em>range scan ✓ &nbsp;/&nbsp; writes harder ✗</em></div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
